---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';
import ConvertDoor from '../components/ConvertDoor.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why would I want the bigger file?',
    a: 'Because PNG is the format the rest of the world agrees on: editors open it, portals accept it, print queues take it, and it stores the picture exactly. If a WebP has to be edited, archived or handed to a program that refuses WebP, the PNG is the safe copy to work from.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the transparency survive?',
    a: 'Yes. PNG holds see-through pixels, so a transparent WebP converts with its transparency intact, pixel for pixel.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will the picture look better as a PNG?',
    a: 'No. The PNG stores the picture exactly as the WebP decoded it; nothing is added back and nothing is sharpened. What changes is the file: bigger, exact, and readable everywhere.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does my file go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. It is decoded, re-encoded and offered back to you inside this page. Nothing is uploaded.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to convert a WebP to a PNG",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick a WebP", text: "Drop a WebP on the tray, or click to pick one. It stays on this device. A file in the wrong format is pointed at the right door." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Convert to PNG", text: "Press 'Convert to PNG'. There is no quality setting: the WebP is decoded and its pixels, transparency included, are written into a PNG exactly, here in the page." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Download the PNG", text: "Download the PNG; the result line prints the bytes before and after, because the file grows and the picture does not change." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="WebP to PNG: convert a WebP to a PNG, free, in your browser · Benchzy" description="Convert one WebP to a PNG inside your browser: an exact, lossless copy of the picture, transparency kept, for editors and doors that refuse WebP. The file grows; the picture does not change. No upload, no account.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">Image tools · convert</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">WebP to PNG</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        A WebP is a website's file. The moment it has to be edited, printed, archived or
        sent through a door that refuses WebP, it needs to become something the rest of the
        world reads. This door converts one WebP to a PNG, here in your browser: every
        pixel written exactly, transparency kept, and the trade stated before you run, the
        file gets bigger and the picture does not change. A whole folder at once is the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/batch-convert-images/">batch convert page</a>;
        the reverse trip is <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/png-to-webp/">PNG to WebP</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <StepsStrip who="For one WebP that has to become a working file: editing, printing, archiving, or a portal that takes PNG and not WebP." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick a WebP' }, { n: '02', t: 'Convert to PNG' }, { n: '03', t: 'Download the PNG' }]} />

    <ConvertDoor from="webp" to="png" note="The wrapper changes, and the picture does not. The decoded picture is written into the PNG pixel for pixel, see-through parts included: nothing added, nothing sharpened. Expect the file to grow, often a lot; the result line prints both sizes." />

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The WebP is decoded by the open-source jSquash codecs and the decoded picture is
          written as a PNG, losslessly, the same engine the batch convert page runs. The
          first run loads the image program (about 2 MB, one time only). The download is how
          we protect your privacy: the program comes to your device and does the work there,
          so your file never leaves it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          Whatever the WebP itself lost when it was made stays lost: the PNG is an exact
          copy of the decoded picture, not a restoration. Animated WebP is read as its first
          frame only; this door is for still pictures. And if the file is going back onto a
          website, the WebP you started with was already the right weight; the
          <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/png-to-webp/">PNG to WebP door</a> is
          the one that lightens PNGs for pages.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/convert-image.js';
  </script>
</Base>
